Chapter: FileSystem
  cites Foreword chapter Path
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 The FileSystem effect provides open-file, read-all, close-file, read-file,
 read-text, and write-file.
 To open-file gives a linear FileHandle for the named path.
 To read-all gives the full contents and an updated FileHandle as a Pair.
 To close-file consumes a linear FileHandle.
 To read-file gives the full text contents of the named path.
 To read-text gives the full text contents of the named path, through a handler.
 To write-file writes text to the named path and answers whether the write landed.

 A caution the names do not show: read-file is ALSO a compiler builtin that
 reads the serial port, and at an application site the builtin wins over the
 effect op. A `with FileSystem` handler over read-file would discharge the
 row at type level while the call went to serial at runtime. read-text exists
 because of that: it is a pure effect op, absent from the builtin tables, so
 a bare read-text always reaches the handler that services it. A layer that
 holds the block device services read-text and write-file over the disk and
 re-emits [Device.Block]; the program above it carries FileSystem.Read and
 never block access. That is the attenuation: holding FileSystem does not
 grant the disk.

 We say:

Section: Definitions

  effect FileSystem where
    open-file  : Text -> [FileSystem.Read] linear FileHandle
    read-all   : linear FileHandle -> [FileSystem.Read] Pair Text (linear FileHandle)
    close-file : linear FileHandle -> [FileSystem.Write] Nothing
    read-file  : Text -> [FileSystem.Read] Text
    read-text  : Text -> [FileSystem.Read] Text
    write-file : Text, Text -> [FileSystem.Write] Boolean

Section: Path Utilities

  fs-read-file : Text, Text -> [FileSystem.Read] Text
  fs-read-file (dir) (filename) = read-text (path-join dir filename)

  fs-write-file : Text, Text, Text -> [FileSystem.Write] Boolean
  fs-write-file (dir) (filename) (content) = write-file (path-join dir filename) content

  fs-sibling : Text, Text -> Text
  fs-sibling (filepath) (new-name) = path-join (path-parent filepath) new-name

  fs-with-extension : Text, Text -> Text
  fs-with-extension (filepath) (ext) =
    let base = path-join (path-parent filepath) (path-stem filepath)
    in base & "." & ext

Section: Default Servicer

 The compiler installs these two functions as the DEFAULT handler for
 read-text and write-file when a program performs a FileSystem op with no
 `with` of its own. A hand-emitted stub checks the
 program's cap-filesystem-read / cap-filesystem-write bit, sets the kernel
 fs-elevated cell so the block device answers even though the program
 holds only FileSystem.Read and never Device.Block, calls one of these,
 and clears the cell. The DIRECTION bit is enforced by that stub; the path
 SCOPE is enforced by fat16 itself (fat16-scope-admits). So a program
 granted FileSystem.Read that performs write-file is refused at the stub,
 which is the guarantee this makes real: holding FileSystem does not grant
 the disk, and holding it read-only does not grant a write.

 This is why FileSystem cites Fat16: the default filesystem IS fat16, and
 a program that cites the effect must carry the backend that services it.
 These functions are called only from the compiler-emitted stub (by name,
 over the handler table), never from Codex source, so their Device.Block
 row is discharged by the elevation, not by the program above them.

  fat16-servicer-read : Text -> [Device.Block] Text
  fat16-servicer-read (path) = act
    vol <- fat16-boot-volume
    when fat16-read-text vol path
      is Just (t) -> t
      is None -> ""
  end

  fat16-servicer-write : Text, Text -> [Device.Block] Boolean
  fat16-servicer-write (path) (content) = fat16-write-file path content

